## VERIFIED FINDINGS

### 1. The gate that controls "Next: Generate"'s `disabled` attribute

`frontend/app/src/pages/GenerateAds/index.tsx:1343-1354`:

```js
const canProceed = useMemo(() => {
  if (step === 'campaign') return !!selections.campaignId;
  if (step === 'products') {
    return selections.productIds.length > 0
        || selections.mediaIds.length > 0
        || selections.campaignKind === 'brand';
  }
  return true;
}, [step, selections]);
```

Wired to the button at `index.tsx:1436`: `<Button onClick={next} ... isDisabled={!canProceed}>Next: {...}</Button>`.

### 2. What a user must select

For a non-brand-kind campaign: at least one product **or** one media item (`selections.productIds.length > 0 || selections.mediaIds.length > 0`). Brand-kind campaigns bypass this entirely (comment at `index.tsx:1345-1347` explains why — brand campaigns fall back to `brand_match` media server-side).

Clicking an actual tile does satisfy this correctly: `Step2Picker.tsx:1198-1221` `toggleProduct()` calls `onChange({ productIds: [...value.productIds, id] })`, which flows straight into `selections` and therefore into `canProceed`. Verified this wiring is intact — no product-side defect in the state update path.

### 3. What j3/j4 actually click — TEST DEFECT confirmed

`.claude/skills/ui-smoke/suites/journeys.js:455-469`, `selectFirstPickerTile()`:

```js
const tiles = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('button')).filter((b) => {
  const w = b.getBoundingClientRect().width;
  return w >= 140 && w <= 200 && (b.innerText || '').trim().length > 0;
});
const unselected = tiles.find((b) => !(b.innerText || '').includes('✓')) || tiles[0];
```

This queries **every `<button>` on the whole page**, unscoped to the picker, filtered only by pixel width and non-empty text. That width band (140–200px) collides with the sidebar's brand switcher button:

- `frontend/app/src/shell/Sidebar.tsx:23,28` — sidebar is `w="240px"` with `px={5}` (20px each side) → 200px of inner content width.
- `frontend/app/src/shell/BrandPicker.tsx:80` — `<MenuButton as={Button} ... w="full" ...>` fills that 200px, landing exactly on the filter's inclusive upper bound (`w <= 200`).
- `frontend/app/src/shell/BrandPicker.tsx:84-86` — the button's rendered text is `Active brand` uppercased via Chakra `textTransform="uppercase"`, which is why `innerText` reads `"ACTIVE BRAND"` (CSS text-transform affects `innerText`, not `textContent` — the suite's own comment at `journeys.js:372-376` documents this exact quirk elsewhere in the file).
- `frontend/app/src/shell/PipelineShell.tsx:15-18` — `<Sidebar>` (rendered as `<aside>`, `Sidebar.tsx:27`) is mounted **before** the main content `<Box flex={1}>` in the DOM, so in document order the sidebar's button comes before any `ProductTile`/`MediaTile`.

Result: `tiles.find()` picks the sidebar's "Active brand" menu button as the "unselected tile," clicks it, and gets `{ ok: true, title: "ACTIVE BRAND" }`. Clicking it only toggles open the Chakra brand-switcher `<Menu>` — it sets no `productIds`/`mediaIds`. So `canProceed` correctly never flips true, and both journeys fail exactly as observed:
- j3 (`journeys.js:1263-1286`): `tile.ok` is true (it "found" the sidebar button), so it waits for Next to enable and times out → `"tile=ACTIVE BRAND state={disabled:true}"`.
- j4 (`journeys.js:1338-1348`): same helper, same non-selection, so `clickNext()` (`journeys.js:431-439`, which explicitly requires `!el.disabled`) finds nothing clickable → `"could not click Next off the products step" / actual: miss`.

Secondary, related defect in the same helper: `MediaTile` is `160px`→ no, `RibbonPicker.tsx:310` gives `MediaTile` a width of **120px**, which is *below* the filter's `w >= 140` lower bound. So even after fixing the sidebar collision, `selectFirstPickerTile()` would never find a media-only tile on a media-only catalog — a second, narrower selector defect worth fixing in the same pass.

### 4. Why lint didn't catch it

`harness/selector-lint.js:92-149` only walks each suite's declared `pages[].selectors[]` string literals and checks they exist verbatim in frontend source (`readSource`/`existsAnywhereInSrc`, lines 55-81, 140-147). `selectFirstPickerTile()` is not a declared string selector at all — it's imperative `page.evaluate()` DOM code (geometry + `innerText` heuristics) buried inside a helper function. Lint has no visibility into helper-function bodies, only the literal `selectors` arrays in the `pages:` config (e.g. `journeys.js:576-586`). There is nothing to preflight here because nothing here is a "selector" in lint's vocabulary.

**Corrected selector**: scope the query to exclude the sidebar, which is a real, source-verified landmark (`Sidebar.tsx:27`, `as="aside"`):

```js
const tiles = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('button')).filter((b) => {
  if (b.closest('aside')) return false;               // excludes BrandPicker/nav chrome
  const w = b.getBoundingClientRect().width;
  return w >= 100 && w <= 200 && (b.innerText || '').trim().length > 0;  // 100 to also catch 120px MediaTile
});
```

(Widening the lower bound to 100–120 fixes the MediaTile miss too.)

### 5. VERDICT

**Test defect, not product defect.** `canProceed` (`index.tsx:1343-1354`) is implemented correctly and `toggleProduct`'s state wiring (`Step2Picker.tsx:1198-1221`) is intact and correct. The failure is entirely inside `selectFirstPickerTile()` in `journeys.js:455-469`, which clicks the sidebar's brand-switcher button instead of a product/media tile because of an unscoped `querySelectorAll('button')` plus a width heuristic that coincidentally also matches the sidebar chrome.

**Can a real human complete the wizard on staging today?** High confidence: **yes**. A human clicks an actual product/media thumbnail visually — they cannot mistake the sidebar's "Active brand" dropdown for a product tile, and I traced the real click handler (`toggleProduct`) through to the exact state (`selections.productIds`) that the disabled-gate reads, with no divergence found. Nothing in this trace touches money/generation logic; both failing journeys die before the products step is ever meaningfully exercised, which is consistent with the test defect rather than a broken gate. I did not personally click through staging in a browser to observe this live — this verdict is based on full static trace of the gate condition, the tile click handler, and the harness code, not a live UI reproduction — but every piece of that trace is internally consistent with the exact telemetry reported (`tile=ACTIVE BRAND`, `disabled:true`).

## Delegation note
Per standing instructions, initial tracing of all three areas (ProductAds/index.tsx, the backend routes, journeys.js) was delegated to local Grok CLI (`grok-4.6`, `--sandbox read-only --always-approve`, medium effort) run in parallel against both repos — zero Anthropic tokens spent on the reading. I then personally re-verified every load-bearing citation below by re-reading the actual source (shown as "verified" quotes), and did the additional tracing (results.json, mintTestToken.js, BrandContext.tsx, run.js) myself since it required cross-referencing a live artifact.

---

## 1. `/product-ads` default status filter

**VERIFIED**, `frontend/app/src/pages/ProductAds/index.tsx`: there is no status-filter state at all. `useState` at the page level is `products/loading/err/selectedCategoryId` (:249-258); the expansion panel's only filters are `selectedCampaignId` and `variantFilter` (:1806-1809). Draft/approved/exported is a **client-side display grouping**, not a query filter:
```178:188:frontend/app/src/pages/ProductAds/index.tsx
export function sectionFor(ad: ExpansionAd): AdSection {
  if (ad.metaSyncStatus === 'synced') return 'exported';
  if (ad.approved) return 'approved';
  return 'draft';
}
```
So **draft ads are never excluded by default** on the primary surface — the historical "done vs draft" trap does not reproduce here.

However, ads only appear after **expanding a specific product's row** — `GET /api/catalog/:productId/ads-detail` (:329-337) — and within that expansion, the campaign selector defaults to the **first campaign only**, not "All Campaigns":
```1806:1809:frontend/app/src/pages/ProductAds/index.tsx
const [selectedCampaignId, setSelectedCampaignId] = useState<string | null>(
  expansion.campaigns[0]?.campaignId ?? null
);
```
If the 3 ads' campaign isn't campaigns[0], a human would need one extra click ("All Campaigns") — a real but minor UX rough edge, not an invisibility bug.

## 2. API call + backend handler + projection trap

**VERIFIED**, `routes/catalog.js:679-736` (`GET /api/catalog/:id/ads-detail`):
```684:708:routes/catalog.js
const filter = {
  brandId:   brandObjectId,
  productId: new mongoose.Types.ObjectId(productId),
  status:    { $ne: 'archived' }
};
```
Only `archived` is excluded — **`draft` matches**. The `$project` (:723-736) includes `status`, `renderUrl`, `platformFormat`, `kind`, `template`; it **omits `visionQc`** (dropped from the JSON payload only, not from the result set — the ad row itself is still returned). `previewImageUrl` doesn't exist anywhere in this repo.

The legacy `/ads` page hits a different handler, `GET /api/ads` (`routes/ads.js:3198-3253`):
```3215:3218:routes/ads.js
const filter = { brandId: new mongoose.Types.ObjectId(String(brandId)) };
...
if (req.query.status)        filter.status         = req.query.status;
```
No default status exclusion — status is whatever the client sends.

**Tenancy**: `Ad` has no `advertiserId` field (`models/Ad.js:29`). Both routes gate tenancy at the `CatalogProduct`/`Brand` level (`tenantFilter`/`assertBrandInTenant`), not on the `Ad` document itself.

## 3. Would the 3 known ads be returned?

**VERIFIED, yes — on both surfaces, by the query logic alone.** `status:'draft'` doesn't hit `{$ne:'archived'}`; nothing matches on `renderUrl`/`visionQc`/`template`/`platformFormat`. The **only** conditions that could exclude them: wrong `productId` (ads-detail is product-scoped), a brand/tenant mismatch, or falling off the 60/50-row recency cap (irrelevant — 3 freshly-created ads sort first).

## 4. How j6 decides "state=empty" — and whether it's reading stale/pre-load state

**VERIFIED**, `.claude/skills/ui-smoke/suites/journeys.js:1611-1648`:
```1611:1612
await run.goto('/ads');
```
```1634:1648
const gridState = await waitUntil(async () => {
  if (await run.hasText('No rendered ads yet')) return 'empty';
  const n = ...match(/(\d+)\s+creatives?/i)...
  if (n && n > 0) return 'loaded';
  return null;
}, { timeoutMs: 25000 });
```
j6 navigates to **`/ads`, the legacy do-not-develop gallery** — not `/product-ads`. (j1 and j7 do go to `/product-ads`, confirmed at :659 and :1731.) It never touches a brand picker, never sets `brandId`/`advertiserId`, and never waits for a loading spinner to clear (contrast j1, which explicitly calls `waitGone(run, 'Loading products…', 30000)` at :681-682). In principle this could race a first-paint "empty" string, but on `/ads` the empty copy is gated behind `!loading` and `loading` starts `false` then flips `true` inside the fetch effect (`frontend/app/src/pages/Ads/index.tsx:543-547, 749-759`), and `goto()` uses `networkidle2`, so in practice the fetch has almost certainly already resolved by the time j6 checks. This is **not primarily a stale-render race**.

## 5. Verdict

**The skip is a test defect, not evidence the product is hiding the ads — two independent, verified causes:**

**(A) Wrong page — fully verified.** j6 tests `/ads` (legacy, marked do-not-develop in project notes), not `/product-ads` (the primary surface six real surfaces use). Even a passing j6 here would say nothing about what a real operator sees.

**(B) Almost certainly the wrong brand — mechanism verified, one link inferred.** This specific run's `results.json` shows it started `2026-08-21T02:51:19Z`, **~12 minutes after** the 3 ads were created (`02:39:26Z`), with `budget.dryRun:true, ceilingUsd:0` — i.e. this run never generated anything; whatever run *did* generate the Pelagic ads was a **separate, earlier `Run.start()`/`mintSession()` call** with its own browser context and localStorage. `run.js` calls `run.start()` exactly once per invocation, before iterating all journeys (`run.js:~500` loop, mint at `run.js:692-693`), and absent an explicit `--brand`, `scripts/mintTestToken.js` silently defaults to **`brands[0]` sorted by name** for the resolved advertiser:
```311:317:liquidretail_backend/scripts/mintTestToken.js
// Same sort /api/me uses (name asc) — first is a stable default for
// single-brand tenants; multi-brand operators should pass --brand-id
// after --list-brands.
brandId = String(brands[0]._id);
```
`BrandContext.tsx:88-100` then keeps whatever `brand_id` the mint seeded, as long as it's valid for the advertiser. I could not find a CLI-args log or shell history proving whether `--brand` was passed on this run's invocation, and I have no DB access from this sandbox to confirm the "Sales Demos" advertiser actually holds multiple brands / that Pelagic Gear isn't alphabetically first — **that specific link is inference**, not directly verified. But the mechanism itself (silent brands[0] fallback, separate session, no brand-pin, no brand assertion anywhere in j6) is fully verified and is by far the more parsimonious explanation than "the product hid a draft ad," since neither surface's query logic excludes `draft`.

**Bottom line:** no code path was found on either `/product-ads` or `/ads` that reproduces the historical status-filter trap — drafts are included by default everywhere traced. The 3 delivered Pelagic Gear ads would be visible to a real human on `/product-ads` right now, provided they have Pelagic Gear selected as the active brand and expand the right product row (possibly one extra "All Campaigns" click). The fix belongs to the harness, not the product: point j6 at `/product-ads`, and either pin `--brand <pelagicGearId>` explicitly or have the journey read back and assert the active brand before judging "empty" — an un-pinned, un-asserted brand context is exactly the kind of harness blind spot that produces a false "invisible ads" report while the pipeline actually delivered correctly.
